A||||3||ponya||Brakes - totally miffed now!||||5||
Z||000000||ponya||11-06-2000||07:21 AM||||I'll try to make this a short as possible, but you need to know what's happening to possibly help. This car is an 86SE and here is what we have done to the brakes. This weekend we did a Pontiac upgrade on the rear brakes, before installing a new E brake cable. The rears have had the calipers, rotors and pads replaced. The fronts have had the right caliper replaced, the rotors turned and new pads installed. The upgrade went without a hitch. Got the E Brake installed. We even put in a new Master cylinder. Now came the time to bleed the brakes. To shorten the story, we used every method possible, gravity, pressure and the old fashioned step on the brake pedal to bleed the brakes. What we wound up with is a mushy pedal. The brakes stop the car in normal situations, but in an emergency situation, I would not trust them. <BR>We thought the Master Cyl. might be bad, so we changed it out and went through the whole bleeding process for the next two days, and exactly the same results. Mushy pedal. The car does not go right or left when the brakes are applied so I figure that the front brakes are grabbing at the same time. The pedal does not go down to the floor if you hold down on the brakes, its just that when you push down in an emerg situation, you don't get the response you should get. I can't think of any other way to describe the problem. If any one has some suggestion as to what we are doing wrong or what we can do to fix this problem, we would really apprecite it. Thanks for your patience with this post.     Bob||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||11-06-2000||08:49 AM||||Bob, <P>Just a suggestion, since you have new front & rear brake pads and new or reconditioned rotors and the brakes do function, drive the car in moderate situations to get the new pads to seat with the rotors.<BR>Quite frequently total brake system component replacement (ie: rotors and pads)do result in a 'spongy pedal' until seated and slightly glazed. Normally the condition will disappear in a couple of days||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||11-06-2000||10:25 AM||||Did you replace the rubber brake lines? If one is going bad it will make the pedal feel mushy. Since you say it stops evenly (left vs right) it could be a rear line. <BR>Just another thing to check.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fldevel||11-06-2000||12:22 PM||||most fieros ive driven kinda have mushy brakes .. well it seems you have to apply more pressure to the pedel anyway ..now im no mechanic but arent the brakes vaccume assisted .. may want to look for a vaccume leak ||||reg||1
